---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Teaching">
  <h1>Teaching &amp; Mentoring</h1>
  <p>
    I love teaching, and helping people discover what excites them in
    STEM. Here's where I've gotten to do that, from a Boston high school
    to Berkeley classrooms and the lab bench.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Reader</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">UC Berkeley &middot; Fall 2026</p>
    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.1rem;">
      Biological Performance of Materials
    </p>
    <p class="pub-meta">BIOE/MSE C118/C208 &middot; Instructor: Dr. Kevin Healy &middot; 30 students</p>
    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 0.1rem;">
      Clinical Need-Based Therapy Solutions
    </p>
    <p class="pub-meta">BIOE 252 &middot; Instructor: Dr. Syed Hossainy &middot; ~100 students</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        In both courses, I organize student teams and coordinate their design
        projects addressing unmet clinical needs, and I grade coursework and
        team projects.
      </li>
      <li>For BIOE 252, I also coordinate guest lecturers from around the country.</li>
      <li>
        I have also delivered four guest lectures in Biological Performance of
        Materials (2023&ndash;2026), including two on hydrogels, one on polymer
        viscoelasticity, and an introduction to polymers.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Graduate Student Instructor</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">UC Berkeley &middot; Fall 2022</p>
    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.1rem;">
      Biological Performance of Materials
    </p>
    <p class="pub-meta">BIOE/MSE C118/C208 &middot; Instructor: Dr. Kevin Healy</p>
    <p>
      For a class of 45 students, I led a weekly discussion section,
      held office hours, and graded homework and exams. I also evaluated
      about 10 team design projects, in which students proposed biomaterial
      solutions to unmet clinical needs through a pitch, two presentations,
      and a 15-page report.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Mentoring in the Lab</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Kevin Healy Lab, UC Berkeley &middot; 2022 &ndash; 2026</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Undergraduate researcher (2023&ndash;2024):</strong> integrated
        hydrogels into a microphysiological system to study FAP migration in a
        physiologically relevant model; now a PhD student at Johns Hopkins
        University.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Master of Translational Medicine capstone team (4 students,
        2024&ndash;2025):</strong> characterized hydrogel rheology to assess
        injectability and conducted a market analysis for rotator cuff injury
        indications.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visiting PhD student from Spain (2024):</strong> fabricated
        carbon nanotube&ndash;HyA hydrogel composites, tested their
        conductivity, and assessed the beating behavior of iPSC-derived
        cardiomyocytes encapsulated in them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Three first-year PhD rotation students (2022&ndash;2026):</strong>
        developed a degradation-based workflow to isolate cells from HyA
        hydrogels, optimized rheology workflows to prevent sample evaporation
        during long-duration tests, and built machine learning&ndash;based
        screening workflows for earlier identification of FAP fate.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>I also trained dozens of other students and lab members on equipment and methods.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Resident Assistant</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Northeastern University &middot; August 2017 &ndash; July 2019</p>
    <p>
      As a live-in mentor for about 45 first-year women each year pursuing careers
      in STEM, I helped residents set goals, connect with
      scientists and professors, and access resources to support their studies and research. I also designed
      programs like Q&amp;A panels with graduate students and faculty,
      leadership and team-building workshops, and faculty mentorship
      events.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Advancement Via Individual Determination (AVID)</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Volunteer Tutor &middot; Fenway High School &middot; September 2018 &ndash; December 2018</p>
    <p>
      At Fenway High School in Boston, I led a weekly group of 6 to 8
      students in AVID, a program that prepares students from
      underrepresented and low-income backgrounds for college. Rather than
      handing out answers, I taught students to help each other work through
      challenging math and science problems by asking good questions and
      thinking critically. Hearing students worry about whether a school
      like Northeastern, just a five-minute walk away, was within reach
      made me want to break down those barriers to STEM.
    </p>
    <a
      class="btn secondary"
      href="https://www.avid.org/"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Visit AVID
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="home-cta">
    <a class="btn" href="/community/">See My Community Work</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
